---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../components/StatusChip.astro';

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Ask the plan | Seculana"
  description="See how Seculana turns a deterministic financial model into scenarios, exact solves, searches, optimizations, and sensitivity analysis."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main" class="deep-page">
    <section class="deep-hero decision-hero section-shell">
      <a class="back-link" href={`${home}#questions`}>← Back to the story</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">From calculation to decision</p>
      <h1>Ask the plan.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A spreadsheet usually starts with inputs and ends with outputs. Seculana can keep the
        financial system fixed, then deliberately change assumptions, solve backward for a target,
        search a policy space, or ask what matters most.
      </p>

      <div class="decision-ribbon" aria-label="Ways to ask a Seculana model questions">
        <div><strong>What if?</strong><span>scenario</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><strong>What exactly works?</strong><span>solve / best</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><strong>How does it vary?</strong><span>explore</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><strong>Which policy wins?</strong><span>optimize</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><strong>What drives it?</strong><span>sensitivity</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--ink">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">1 · Branch the plan</p>
          <h2>Change one assumption without copying the model.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            A scenario replaces or adds declarations over the same model. Sam and Alex can endure a
            market crash, spend less, or combine both, while the underlying tax, account, and time
            semantics stay the same.
          </p>
          <StatusChip tone="live" label="Implemented" />
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel">
          <p class="code-panel-label">examples/sam-and-alex.sec</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">scenario</span> Crash &#123;
    let Growth = &#123; 2027: -25%, 2028: -8%, else: 6% &#125;
&#125;

<span class="code-key">scenario</span> Frugal &#123;
    let Living = @Living * (1 + Inflation)
        starting $46,000
&#125;

<span class="code-key">scenario</span> CrashButFrugal based on Crash &#123;
    let Living = @Living * (1 + Inflation)
        starting $46,000
&#125;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell scenario-fork" aria-label="One model branching into three scenarios">
        <div class="scenario-base"><span>Base model</span><strong>one source of truth</strong></div>
        <div class="scenario-lines" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
        <div class="scenario-branches">
          <div><span>Crash</span><strong>returns change</strong></div>
          <div><span>Frugal</span><strong>spending changes</strong></div>
          <div><span>Crash + Frugal</span><strong>scenario based on scenario</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--proof">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading--wide">
          <p class="eyebrow">2 · Reverse the arrow</p>
          <h2>Sometimes the unknown belongs on the left.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Instead of guessing an input and seeing the answer, <code>solve</code> finds the input
            that makes two sides equal. <code>best</code> finds the value in a range that minimizes
            or maximizes an objective.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="inverse-grid">
          <article>
            <p class="question-word">Target → required input</p>
            <h3>What salary takes home $100,000 after tax?</h3>
            <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
              <pre><code><span class="code-key">let</span> GrossNeeded =
    <span class="code-key">solve</span> g from $0 to $500,000
    so that
        g - Tax.IncomeTax(Province, g, 0, Indexation)
        == $100,000</code></pre>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="question-word">Range → best point</p>
            <h3>What pension split produces the lowest household tax?</h3>
            <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
              <pre><code><span class="code-key">let</span> Split =
    <span class="code-key">best</span> s from 0 to Pension / 2
    <span class="code-key">for lowest</span> HouseholdTax(s)</code></pre>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="inverse-note">
          <strong>Same evaluator, many tries.</strong>
          <span>The search varies the temporary decision. The financial model still decides whether each try is valid and what it produces.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">3 · Map the landscape</p>
          <h2>Explore before you optimize.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            An exploration varies one or two model values and shows the outcomes at each point.
            Lise and Omar can see how an RRSP-meltdown amount changes both the after-tax estate and
            lifetime tax before asking an optimizer to choose.
          </p>
          <StatusChip tone="live" label="Implemented" />
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
          <p class="code-panel-label">examples/lise-and-omar.sec</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">explore</span> Melt &#123;
    <span class="code-key">vary</span> Meltdown from 0 to 40000 step 10000
    <span class="code-key">show</span> EstateToday, TotalTax
&#125;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell exploration-strip" aria-label="Illustrative exploration points">
        <div><span>$0</span><small>policy point</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>$10k</span><small>run model</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>$20k</span><small>run model</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>$30k</span><small>run model</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div><span>$40k</span><small>compare outcomes</small></div>
      </div>
      <p class="section-shell fine-print">
        The strip shows the written policy points, not their numerical results. Seculana evaluates the real model at every point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--showcase">
      <div class="section-shell">
        <div class="section-heading section-heading--wide">
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">4 · Choose across the whole plan</p>
          <h2>Optimization is policy search wrapped around the model.</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            In Lise and Omar, the withdrawal order itself is a decision. With five sources there are
            120 possible orders. The optimizer can choose the order that leaves the largest estate
            while requiring that the household never runs short.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="optimization-layout">
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel-label">One decision: the order</p>
            <pre><code><span class="code-key">optimize</span> BestOrder &#123;
    <span class="code-key">vary order of</span> Living
    <span class="code-key">for highest</span> EstateToday
    <span class="code-key">keep</span> YearsShort == 0
&#125;</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel-label">Two decisions together</p>
            <pre><code><span class="code-key">optimize</span> BestPlan &#123;
    <span class="code-key">vary order of</span> Living
    <span class="code-key">vary</span> Meltdown from 0 to 40000 step 5000
    <span class="code-key">for highest</span> EstateToday
    <span class="code-key">keep</span> YearsShort == 0
    tries 400
&#125;</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="optimizer-logic" aria-label="Optimization loop">
          <div><span>Propose</span><strong>a policy</strong></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>Run</span><strong>the real model</strong></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>Check</span><strong>constraints</strong></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>Rank</span><strong>the objective</strong></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>Search</span><strong>again</strong></div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine-print fine-print--light">
          The current optimizer is a deterministic local search. It can find a strong point near where it searched; it does not claim a mathematical proof of the global optimum.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-section--roadmap">
      <div class="section-shell deep-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">5 · Find the fragile assumptions</p>
          <h2>Which guess moves the answer most?</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">
            Sensitivity moves one assumption at a time while the rest of the plan stays as written.
            Its tornado ranks the swing, its spider plot shows shape, and it can also measure
            contribution to variance when randomness is present.
          </p>
          <StatusChip tone="live" label="Implemented" />
        </div>
        <div class="code-panel code-panel--light">
          <p class="code-panel-label">examples/lise-and-omar.sec</p>
          <pre><code><span class="code-key">sensitivity</span> Drivers &#123;
    <span class="code-key">of</span> EstateToday
    <span class="code-key">vary</span> CPI, Returns, CashRate
    <span class="code-key">vary</span> Meltdown from 0 to 40000
&#125;</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-shell sensitivity-bars" aria-label="Conceptual sensitivity ranking">
        <div><span>largest swing</span><i style="--w: 94%"></i><strong>ranked first</strong></div>
        <div><span>next</span><i style="--w: 68%"></i><strong>ranked by effect</strong></div>
        <div><span>smaller</span><i style="--w: 39%"></i><strong>less influential</strong></div>
      </div>
      <p class="section-shell fine-print">
        These bars explain the tornado concept; they are not Lise and Omar's measured sensitivity values. Authentic result graphics will come from Seculana itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section closing-section">
      <div class="section-shell closing-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The pattern</p>
          <h2>Propose. Run. Verify.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="closing-copy">
          <p>
            Scenarios, solves, explorations, optimizations, and sensitivity all gain their meaning
            from the same financial model. The question changes. The authority does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            The next layer is uncertainty: keep the model and the decision machinery, then let the
            assumptions themselves move across reproducible futures.
          </p>
          <a class="button button--solid" href={`${home}#proof`}>Back to the main story</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer section-shell">
    <p><strong>Seculana</strong> · Ask the plan.</p>
    <p>Every Seculana snippet on this page comes from the current repository or manual.</p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
